1
00:00:00,119 --> 00:00:01,529
Will this customer click on the ad?

2
00:00:01,968 --> 00:00:03,111
Is this email spam?

3
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:05,890
What word will a language model write next?

4
00:00:06,316 --> 00:00:08,856
Machine learning lives with uncertainty.

5
00:00:09,281 --> 00:00:11,316
We cannot know the answer before it happens.

6
00:00:11,755 --> 00:00:15,319
But to compute with uncertainty, we first need numbers.

7
00:00:15,757 --> 00:00:19,454
The tool that turns chance into numbers is called a random variable.

8
00:00:20,373 --> 00:00:27,208
In this video you will learn what a random variable really is: not something mysterious, but a simple rule.

9
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:34,588
You will build random variables from coins and dice, ask questions about them, and watch them in a computer simulation.

10
00:00:35,013 --> 00:00:38,524
You only need counting and the idea of a function.

11
00:00:38,963 --> 00:00:43,138
This is the first video about axiom D, the rules of probability.

12
00:00:44,859 --> 00:00:46,069
Start with an experiment.

13
00:00:46,468 --> 00:00:55,670
In probability, an experiment is anything with an uncertain result: flipping a coin, rolling a die, picking a random email.

14
00:00:56,109 --> 00:00:58,290
Each possible result is called an outcome.

15
00:00:58,716 --> 00:01:01,654
A coin has two outcomes: heads and tails.

16
00:01:03,173 --> 00:01:06,351
Now roll two dice, a blue one and a green one.

17
00:01:06,803 --> 00:01:08,718
The blue die shows one to six.

18
00:01:09,170 --> 00:01:11,524
The green die also shows one to six.

19
00:01:11,962 --> 00:01:13,891
Every pair is a different outcome.

20
00:01:14,316 --> 00:01:16,923
Six times six gives thirty six outcomes.

21
00:01:17,361 --> 00:01:18,239
Here they are in a grid.

22
00:01:18,665 --> 00:01:23,465
Each cell is one outcome, for example blue three and green four.

23
00:01:24,406 --> 00:01:25,137
Notice something.

24
00:01:25,563 --> 00:01:27,584
Outcomes are not always numbers.

25
00:01:28,023 --> 00:01:28,901
Heads is a word.

26
00:01:29,326 --> 00:01:30,523
An email is a text.

27
00:01:30,962 --> 00:01:32,159
A photo is a picture.

28
00:01:32,571 --> 00:01:36,427
Even for dice, an outcome is a pair, not one number.

29
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:39,924
But mathematics and computers want numbers.

30
00:01:40,363 --> 00:01:43,076
So we need a way to go from outcomes to numbers.

31
00:01:44,819 --> 00:01:46,309
Here is the key idea.

32
00:01:46,721 --> 00:01:50,431
A random variable is a rule that gives each outcome a number.

33
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:51,388
That is all.

34
00:01:51,814 --> 00:01:52,492
Take the coin.

35
00:01:52,931 --> 00:01:57,186
Let Y be one if the outcome is heads, and zero if it is tails.

36
00:01:57,625 --> 00:01:59,872
Each outcome gets exactly one number.

37
00:02:00,298 --> 00:02:01,588
Y is a random variable.

38
00:02:03,106 --> 00:02:04,263
Back to the two dice.

39
00:02:04,675 --> 00:02:06,710
Let X be the sum of the two dice.

40
00:02:07,135 --> 00:02:10,593
The rule reads the outcome and adds the two faces.

41
00:02:11,045 --> 00:02:14,063
Blue three and green four give seven.

42
00:02:14,489 --> 00:02:16,789
Blue six and green six give twelve.

43
00:02:17,255 --> 00:02:22,880
Fill in the whole grid, and every outcome now has a number from two to twelve.

44
00:02:23,806 --> 00:02:24,989
A word about notation.

45
00:02:25,428 --> 00:02:28,407
We write the rule with a capital letter, X.

46
00:02:28,846 --> 00:02:31,891
One value it can take gets a small letter, x.

47
00:02:32,317 --> 00:02:35,894
So X of blue three, green four equals seven.

48
00:02:36,306 --> 00:02:41,146
And a fun fact: a random variable is neither random nor a variable.

49
00:02:41,585 --> 00:02:42,370
It is a function.

50
00:02:42,795 --> 00:02:45,960
The randomness lives in the outcome, not in the rule.

51
00:02:46,906 --> 00:02:49,991
One experiment can carry many random variables.

52
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:53,382
Take the outcome blue two, green five.

53
00:02:53,834 --> 00:02:55,137
The sum is seven.

54
00:02:55,576 --> 00:02:57,451
The largest face is five.

55
00:02:57,890 --> 00:03:00,948
The difference, green minus blue, is three.

56
00:03:01,401 --> 00:03:05,190
Three different rules, three different numbers, from the very same roll.

57
00:03:05,603 --> 00:03:08,222
We choose the rule that measures what we care about.

58
00:03:09,939 --> 00:03:11,176
Now we can ask questions.

59
00:03:11,601 --> 00:03:16,562
An event is a set of outcomes, such as the outcomes where X is at least ten.

60
00:03:16,974 --> 00:03:17,865
Which cells are they?

61
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:23,104
A sum of ten has three cells, eleven has two, and twelve has one.

62
00:03:23,530 --> 00:03:27,373
So the event X at least ten has six outcomes out of thirty six.

63
00:03:28,306 --> 00:03:31,285
Another question: when is X equal to seven?

64
00:03:31,684 --> 00:03:32,362
Look at the grid.

65
00:03:32,801 --> 00:03:39,596
The sevens lie on one diagonal: one and six, two and five, all the way to six and one.

66
00:03:40,035 --> 00:03:41,285
That is six outcomes too.

67
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:43,133
No other sum has that many.

68
00:03:43,585 --> 00:03:46,218
So seven is the most common sum of two dice.

69
00:03:47,139 --> 00:03:48,230
Some events are extreme.

70
00:03:48,655 --> 00:03:52,738
X equal to one is impossible: the smallest sum is two.

71
00:03:53,176 --> 00:03:56,168
That event is empty, it has no outcomes.

72
00:03:56,607 --> 00:04:01,355
And X at most twelve always happens: all thirty six outcomes are in it.

73
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:05,570
Counting outcomes like this tells us how likely each value is.

74
00:04:06,473 --> 00:04:11,100
With fair dice, every outcome has the same chance, one in thirty six.

75
00:04:11,539 --> 00:04:15,781
So the chance of an event is its number of outcomes divided by thirty six.

76
00:04:16,207 --> 00:04:23,055
The chance that X is at least ten is six over thirty six, about zero point one six seven.

77
00:04:23,494 --> 00:04:28,095
The full table of chances for every value is the topic of video twelve.

78
00:04:29,806 --> 00:04:31,827
Random variables come in two kinds.

79
00:04:32,239 --> 00:04:41,707
A discrete random variable takes separate values that you can list: zero or one for the coin, two to twelve for the dice, or the number of emails you get in a day.

80
00:04:42,133 --> 00:04:43,702
You can count them, one by one.

81
00:04:44,626 --> 00:04:48,788
A continuous random variable can take any value in a range.

82
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:54,905
Think of the height of a random person, like one hundred seventy two point three centimeters.

83
00:04:55,344 --> 00:05:00,876
Or the time you wait for the next bus: two minutes, two point five, two point five one.

84
00:05:01,328 --> 00:05:04,892
Between any two values there are always more values.

85
00:05:05,879 --> 00:05:11,305
For continuous variables, we ask about ranges instead of exact values.

86
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:17,089
For example: what is the chance that the waiting time is between two and five minutes?

87
00:05:17,528 --> 00:05:19,749
The rule idea does not change.

88
00:05:20,161 --> 00:05:22,448
An outcome comes in, a number comes out.

89
00:05:22,874 --> 00:05:25,321
Only the set of possible numbers is different.

90
00:05:27,072 --> 00:05:31,354
We can also see a random variable by running the experiment many times.

91
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:35,450
Each run gives one outcome, and the rule gives one number.

92
00:05:35,875 --> 00:05:37,844
That number is called a sample of X.

93
00:05:38,282 --> 00:05:46,713
Here are the first six runs of a computer simulation: the sums are ten, eleven, nine, eight, three, and eight.

94
00:05:48,239 --> 00:05:53,199
Now run it ten thousand times and count how often each sum appears.

95
00:05:53,638 --> 00:05:55,699
This bar chart is called a histogram.

96
00:05:56,111 --> 00:05:57,361
It has a triangle shape.

97
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:04,037
The middle value, seven, appears most often, about one thousand six hundred eighty times.

98
00:06:04,476 --> 00:06:08,332
Two and twelve are rare, because each has only one outcome.

99
00:06:09,226 --> 00:06:11,340
Compare the simulation with our counting.

100
00:06:11,752 --> 00:06:17,484
The event X at least ten happened in zero point one six three seven of the runs.

101
00:06:17,922 --> 00:06:20,569
Counting gave zero point one six seven.

102
00:06:20,994 --> 00:06:25,050
They are close, but not equal, because samples are random.

103
00:06:25,476 --> 00:06:29,292
With more runs, the share usually gets closer to the counted value.

104
00:06:30,206 --> 00:06:33,131
This is exactly the situation in machine learning.

105
00:06:33,557 --> 00:06:36,456
We almost never know the rule and all the outcomes.

106
00:06:36,868 --> 00:06:40,126
We only see samples, and those samples are our data.

107
00:06:40,551 --> 00:06:44,847
A histogram of the data is our window into the random variable.

108
00:06:45,285 --> 00:06:48,530
The more samples we have, the clearer the picture becomes.

109
00:06:50,226 --> 00:06:53,643
In machine learning, random variables are everywhere.

110
00:06:54,095 --> 00:06:56,063
Pick a random email from your inbox.

111
00:06:56,502 --> 00:07:00,106
The label Y is one if it is spam and zero if it is not.

112
00:07:00,545 --> 00:07:03,603
That is a random variable, exactly like the coin.

113
00:07:04,015 --> 00:07:07,566
A classifier tries to predict Y from the email.

114
00:07:09,106 --> 00:07:11,379
Measurements are random variables too.

115
00:07:11,792 --> 00:07:17,098
A thermometer shows the true temperature plus a small error, called noise.

116
00:07:17,536 --> 00:07:23,215
If the true value is twenty degrees, the reading might be twenty point three, or nineteen point eight.

117
00:07:23,653 --> 00:07:28,148
The noise is a continuous random variable, and so is the reading.

118
00:07:29,672 --> 00:07:32,212
And a data set is a collection of samples.

119
00:07:32,651 --> 00:07:41,680
Each row is one sample of a random vector: a list of random variables measured together, like age, income, and the label.

120
00:07:42,119 --> 00:07:46,893
When a language model writes, the next word is also a random variable.

121
00:07:47,305 --> 00:07:50,470
Each time it generates, we see one sample.

122
00:07:52,032 --> 00:07:56,580
Here is a real example from the book Build a Large Language Model from Scratch.

123
00:07:57,006 --> 00:08:10,490
After the words every effort moves you, a small model gave the next word forward a probability of about zero point five seven, toward about zero point three six, and closer about zero point zero six.

124
00:08:10,915 --> 00:08:15,742
In one thousand samples, forward appeared five hundred eighty two times.

125
00:08:16,181 --> 00:08:19,492
Each sample is one value of the same random variable.

126
00:08:21,239 --> 00:08:21,757
Your turn.

127
00:08:22,236 --> 00:08:23,340
Flip two coins.

128
00:08:23,765 --> 00:08:25,773
Let Z be the number of heads.

129
00:08:26,199 --> 00:08:27,036
Pause the video.

130
00:08:27,435 --> 00:08:31,584
Write down all the outcomes, and give each outcome its value of Z.

131
00:08:32,010 --> 00:08:33,792
Which outcomes give Z equal to one?

132
00:08:34,739 --> 00:08:35,922
There are four outcomes.

133
00:08:36,348 --> 00:08:37,438
Heads heads gives two.

134
00:08:37,864 --> 00:08:38,994
Heads tails gives one.

135
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:40,430
Tails heads gives one.

136
00:08:40,856 --> 00:08:42,345
Tails tails gives zero.

137
00:08:42,784 --> 00:08:47,757
So Z equals one for two outcomes: heads tails and tails heads.

138
00:08:48,169 --> 00:08:51,055
Notice that two different outcomes can give the same number.

139
00:08:51,507 --> 00:08:52,106
That is allowed.

140
00:08:53,639 --> 00:08:54,423
Second question.

141
00:08:54,835 --> 00:08:57,827
Roll two fair dice, and let X be their sum.

142
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:01,245
How many outcomes are in the event X at most three?

143
00:09:01,684 --> 00:09:02,814
And what is its chance?

144
00:09:03,253 --> 00:09:03,958
Pause and try.

145
00:09:04,872 --> 00:09:07,279
A sum of two needs one and one.

146
00:09:07,718 --> 00:09:11,614
A sum of three needs one and two, or two and one.

147
00:09:12,053 --> 00:09:13,489
That is three outcomes.

148
00:09:13,928 --> 00:09:20,364
The chance is three over thirty six, which is one twelfth, about zero point zero eight three.

149
00:09:22,105 --> 00:09:24,698
Let's simulate it in Python with NumPy.

150
00:09:25,097 --> 00:09:30,642
We create a random number generator with a fixed seed, so the run repeats exactly.

151
00:09:31,081 --> 00:09:34,060
We roll two dice ten thousand times.

152
00:09:34,486 --> 00:09:36,999
The random variable X is the sum of each row.

153
00:09:37,424 --> 00:09:42,145
Then we count each sum, and the share of runs where X is at least ten.

154
00:09:43,672 --> 00:09:47,741
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

155
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:53,499
The first rolls give sums ten, eleven, nine, eight, three, and eight.

156
00:09:53,938 --> 00:09:58,246
Seven appeared one thousand six hundred eighty times, the most of all.

157
00:09:58,672 --> 00:10:06,252
The share of X at least ten is zero point one six three seven, close to the counted zero point one six seven.

158
00:10:06,691 --> 00:10:10,520
The coin flips give seven ones and three zeros.

159
00:10:12,239 --> 00:10:12,930
In summary.

160
00:10:13,342 --> 00:10:15,191
An experiment has outcomes.

161
00:10:15,616 --> 00:10:19,127
A random variable is a rule that gives each outcome a number.

162
00:10:19,539 --> 00:10:22,624
One experiment can have many random variables.

163
00:10:23,036 --> 00:10:30,550
An event is a set of outcomes, like X at least ten, and with equally likely outcomes, its chance comes from counting.

164
00:10:31,472 --> 00:10:36,631
Random variables can be discrete or continuous, and we see them through samples.

165
00:10:37,030 --> 00:10:41,725
Labels, noise, and data sets are all random variables.

166
00:10:42,150 --> 00:10:48,706
Next, in video twelve, we write down the full rules of chance: the probability distribution.

167
00:10:49,145 --> 00:10:49,690
See you there.

168
00:10:51,639 --> 00:10:53,460
This video was made by David R.

169
00:10:53,899 --> 00:10:57,131
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

170
00:10:57,689 --> 00:10:58,407
Thanks for watching.
